WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.070
- [Kim] Hi, this is Kim from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.100
And today, I'm learning more
about the First Amendment

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.970
to the U.S. Constitution.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.840
The First Amendment is one of
the most important amendments

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.960
to the Constitution, if
not the most important.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.577
It reads: "Congress shall make no law

00:00:15.577 --> 00:00:18.257
"respecting an establishment of religion,

00:00:18.257 --> 00:00:21.484
"or prohibiting the free exercise thereof;

00:00:21.484 --> 00:00:24.327
"or abridging the freedom
of speech, or of the press;

00:00:24.327 --> 00:00:26.967
"or the right of the people
peaceably to assemble,

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:28.297
"and to petition the government

00:00:28.297 --> 00:00:30.540
"for a redress of grievances."

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:32.350
To learn more about the First Amendment,

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:33.890
I talked to two experts.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:37.020
Erwin Chemerinsky is the Jesse H. Choper

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.950
Distinguished Professor of Law

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:40.730
and Dean of Berkeley Law.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:42.590
Michael McConnell is the Director

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.930
of the Constitutional Law
Center at Stanford Law School

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.720
and a Senior Fellow at
the Hoover Institution.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.390
Professor Chemerinsky,
there's a lot going on

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.697
in the First Amendment.

00:00:52.697 --> 00:00:55.530
Can you tell us a little bit
more about why the framers

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.560
chose to protect these
rights in particular?

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.120
- [Erwin] The historical
background of the First Amendment

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.940
of the Constitution shows why the framers

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.430
wanted to be sure that
all of the liberties

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.800
in the First Amendment were safeguarded.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.450
Let's focus on freedom of
speech and freedom of the press.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.200
In England, there was after
the printing press developed

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.390
licensing so that anyone
who wanted to be able

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.040
to publish anything needed a
license from the government.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.860
Such licensing was seen to be inconsistent

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.340
with freedom of speech;
freedom of the press;

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.450
for that matter: freedom of
thought, freedom of inquiry.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.283
- [Kim] Interesting.

00:01:34.283 --> 00:01:37.520
Okay, so there are a lot
of essential freedoms

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.710
that are packed into this First Amendment,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.020
so much that it's almost
amazing that we're gonna attempt

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.430
to talk about them all in one video.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.320
But if we dial into freedom of speech,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.490
Professor McConnell, what
is freedom of speech?

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:56.180
Does that encompass some
things and not others?

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.310
- [Michael] Freedom of speech
was actually less important

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.640
to the framers than the freedom
with which it was coupled

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.720
which is the freedom of the press.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.730
And the reason for this is that speech

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:12.930
that can reach large audiences
is much more important

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.720
to the individual, but
also dangerous to the state

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:17.660
than mere speech.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:21.460
When you speak, only those
people within hearing range

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.293
can hear you.

00:02:22.293 --> 00:02:26.510
But when you are able to use Gutenberg's

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.670
fantastic new technology
to publish your sentiments

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.340
and distribute them widely,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.140
maybe even over the entire
country or across the Atlantic

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.870
and reaching hundreds
of thousands of people,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.860
now that is powerful.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.380
And no wonder.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:47.240
So you think about how the
American Revolution was won.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.350
This required spreading the word.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.410
It required gaining
converts and telling people

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.290
what their grievances were.

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.710
To a very great extent, this was done

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:59.690
through the mechanism
of the printing press.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:00.760
- [Erwin] When you ask the question of

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.910
what's freedom of speech,
there's implicit within it

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:07.180
the issue of: what do we mean by speech.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.560
Ultimately, the answer to your question is

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:13.610
the First Amendment protecting
speech broadly safeguards

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.130
a right to express one's ideas.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.150
But it's not absolute.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.010
The government can restrict expression

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.040
if there's a compelling interest.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.520
- [Kim] Interesting, can
you say more about that?

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.740
- [Erwin] The Supreme
Court always has been clear

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.000
that freedom of speech is not absolute.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.490
The court has said that
there are certain categories

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:37.920
of speech that are unprotected
by the First Amendment.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.620
Incitement of illegal
activity is a category

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:42.980
of unprotected speech.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.070
The court has said this
requires showing that

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:47.230
the speech was directed at causing

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:48.960
imminent illegal activity,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.670
and there was substantial likelihood

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.800
of imminent illegal activity.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.570
Another example: obscenity is unprotected

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:56.980
by the First Amendment.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.660
The court struggled for
years trying to define

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:01.060
what is obscenity.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.620
Maybe the low point in that is

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.577
when Justice Potter Stewart said,

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:07.290
"I can't define it, but
I know it when I see it."

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:09.540
There are also these categories of speech

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:11.990
where the government can prohibit,

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:13.670
even punish the expression.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:17.550
- [Michael] That's where
most of the limitations

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.500
on the freedom of speech
and of the press come from

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.940
is in order to make sure that we don't

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:27.130
hurt other people's rights
through the use of our own.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.240
So an example of that.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:32.300
The government can prohibit speech

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:35.410
which incites violence against someone.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:40.160
So if you're making a speech
and calling upon the crowd

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.430
to attack somebody's
house or their person,

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:46.080
that can be punished and prevented

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.870
as an incitement to violence.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.340
And you can see how that
follows from the logical idea

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:54.420
of freedom of speech being a natural right

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.320
and therefore limited by
the rights of other people.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.940
- [Kim] So let's turn our attention

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:04.940
toward the freedom of religion
part of the First Amendment.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.710
So the first thing that the amendment says

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.160
is about establishment.

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.853
So what does this
establishment clause prevent?

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.580
- [Erwin] The language of the
First Amendment is important.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.087
It says that "Congress may make no law

00:05:23.087 --> 00:05:26.350
"respecting the
establishment of religion."

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.760
Since 1947, the Supreme
Court has said that

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.270
that also applies to state
and local governments.

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:33.760
And the Supreme Court has said this means

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.350
that the government cannot
act with the purpose

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.120
of advancing religion.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.000
- [Michael] To the founders,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.360
this was a very clear legal concept.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.060
Namely, it was the
established Church of England.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.310
In the statute books, in the law,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.790
the Church of England was referred to as

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:56.670
and I quote "the Church
by law established."

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.140
And what did it mean to be established?

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:02.930
First of all, it meant that
the doctrines of the church,

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:06.540
the 39 Articles of Faith
of the Church of England

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:10.090
were voted upon by Parliament.

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:14.180
So the doctrines, the liturgy, the text

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:16.970
were all adopted by law.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:21.410
The established church was
the government's church.

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:24.980
And it could be used, and
from time to time was used,

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.300
as an instrument of government

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.380
or as an instrument of politics.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:31.180
And this is a way in which the government

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:35.100
is able to have a powerful
influence on the way

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:40.000
in which values and
opinions are inculcated.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.520
It's one of the most important ideas

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.520
of the established church,
especially in the 18th century,

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:53.342
was to teach that there's
actually a religious obligation

00:06:53.342 --> 00:06:58.000
to obey the law and to recognize the king

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:03.000
as a supreme leader in matters
of both church and state.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:07.670
And the framers' experience with this

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:11.560
was extremely powerful.

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:13.670
- [Erwin] That's because
the framers were aware

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:15.700
of the religious persecution

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:18.260
that had gone on in other countries.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.950
They were aware of the evils that occur

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.780
when the government becomes aligned

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.230
with a particular religion.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:28.160
- [Michael] The principal
reason why many of the colonists

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.760
had come to these shores to begin with

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:33.660
was to escape the oppressions
of the established

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:36.840
Church of England back
home and to come to a place

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:39.470
where they would be able to exercise

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:42.050
the freedom of religion for themselves.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:44.490
And the main opponents
of the established church

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:48.240
were not anti-Christian
or anti-religious people.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.440
They were the most religious people.

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.530
And their view was the government should

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:55.850
stay out of our church,
that we will decide

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.760
what we believe for ourselves.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.450
We will control our own church.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:00.900
We will write our own liturgy.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.250
We'll decide what version of the Bible

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:04.642
we're going to use.

00:08:04.642 --> 00:08:07.070
We'll choose our own ministers.

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:10.220
Thank you very much,
government, stay out of it.

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:12.260
Leave us free to practice our religion

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.050
without having this kind
of an establishment.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:18.270
- [Erwin] So for instance,
a county in Kentucky

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.230
required that the 10
Commandments be posted

00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:23.390
in all county buildings.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:25.919
The Supreme Court said the 10 Commandments

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:28.020
are a religious scripture.

00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.910
There's no secular purpose
for having the 10 Commandments

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:33.960
posted in county buildings.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.180
The court declared it unconstitutional.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:39.350
The Supreme Court has said
the government can't act

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:43.200
where the primary effect is to advance

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.750
or to inhibit religion.

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.550
So for example, there can't
be prayer in public schools.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:52.070
Even a voluntary prayer in
public schools in impermissible.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:55.090
Because the court has said
that the primary effect

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:58.810
of having prayer in public
schools is to advance religion.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:02.440
The court has explained that
children will inevitably

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.610
feel pressured to participate.

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:07.130
And this coercion
violates the Constitution.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:11.220
- [Kim] So the First
Amendment then prevents

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:13.830
that kind of intermingling

00:09:13.830 --> 00:09:17.350
of the government and the church.

00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:20.020
This is I guess the key idea of separation

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.790
between church and state.

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:26.547
But it also says that the
"Congress shall make no law

00:09:26.547 --> 00:09:29.590
"prohibiting the free
exercise" of religion.

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.240
So what does that mean?

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.680
- [Michael] Free exercise
of religion was the right

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.500
to practice your own religion.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.690
It didn't keep the government
from setting up a church,

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.100
but it did keep the government

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.700
from requiring you to attend that church,

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.590
maybe even to contribute to the church,

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:49.980
but also kept the government
from preventing you

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:51.620
from worshiping elsewhere.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:54.850
So the establishment clause by and large

00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:57.740
prevents the government
from forcing people

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:59.710
to participate in religion.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:03.010
And the free exercise clause by and large

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.520
prohibits the government
from preventing people

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.560
from practicing their religion.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.570
Those two things work together

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:15.280
to enable everyone to worship God

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.440
in accordance with their own conscience.

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:18.273
- [Kim] Interesting.

00:10:18.273 --> 00:10:21.100
So we mentioned a little bit
about freedom of the press.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.717
But there are two other
aspects of the First Amendment:

00:10:24.717 --> 00:10:27.577
"the right of the people
peaceably to assemble,

00:10:27.577 --> 00:10:29.127
"and to petition the government

00:10:29.127 --> 00:10:31.520
"for a redress of grievances."

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:35.500
So what is included with the
right to peaceably assemble?

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:39.310
Are there any situations where
that might be restricted?

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:40.700
- [Erwin] The Supreme Court has said

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:43.500
that under freedom of
speech, there's a right

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.970
to use government property
for speech purposes.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:49.460
This is also something which entails

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:50.610
the freedom of assembly.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:51.443
And the Supreme Court has said that

00:10:51.443 --> 00:10:54.290
there's certain government
properties that the government

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:56.510
is required to make available for speech:

00:10:56.510 --> 00:10:58.510
sidewalks and parks.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:10:59.910
There's other places where the government

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.320
has more latitude to regulating speech:

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:04.630
school facilities evenings and weekends.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:05.730
There's places where the government

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:08.280
can close entirely to speech:

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:11.420
military bases, areas
outside prisons and jails.

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:14.160
All of these cases have been litigated

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:15.750
under freedom of assembly.

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.350
Some of the earlier cases
explicitly mentioned

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.340
freedom of assembly.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:23.570
But subsequent cases
combined freedom of assembly

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:26.030
into the protection of freedom of speech.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.300
- [Kim] I guess that makes sense.

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:31.140
But what about something
like a march, for example,

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:33.760
that might say block traffic?

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:37.610
That's perhaps a clear case
when there is this tension

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.760
between freedom of speech and assembly

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:44.760
and say public safety if they're
blocking say an ambulance.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.580
How do you resolve that tension?

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:51.210
- [Michael] Sometime in roughly the 1970s,

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:55.010
the court began using
a quite different way

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:58.120
of looking at the free speech question

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:01.490
in which they said that

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.360
laws which regulate or prohibit speech

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:08.530
on the basis of the content of the speech

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:11.910
are, generally speaking, unconstitutional

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:15.160
absent a very important
governmental purpose.

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:18.990
But that laws that are content neutral

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:22.010
and regulate speech on
the basis of its time,

00:12:22.010 --> 00:12:25.490
place, or manner are permitted.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:27.590
So the basic idea here is

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:30.940
the government has regulatory
authority over speech,

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:32.920
but not over what you say,

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:35.270
but just over when you say it,

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:37.510
where you say it, how you say it.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:40.300
- [Kim] All right, so the last
thing in the First Amendment

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.447
is the phrase "petitioning the government

00:12:42.447 --> 00:12:45.570
"for a redress of grievances."

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:49.190
Congress shall make no law
abridging that freedom.

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:50.400
What does this mean?

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:52.360
How would one petition the government

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:55.100
for a redress of grievances?

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:57.150
- [Erwin] There are, of
course, many ways that people

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.350
can petition government for
a redress of grievances.

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:01.240
It's the ability to go and testify

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.820
before our legislative body.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:06.210
It's the ability to communicate
with one's legislators

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.500
or representatives about change.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:11.870
It's basically the ability
to go to the government

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:14.010
and ask it to change its policy.

00:13:14.010 --> 00:13:17.630
There are relatively few
cases just about the right

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.500
to petition government
for redress of grievances.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:21.230
Again, I think the reason for that is

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.950
it's been so subsumed into the protection

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:26.770
of freedom of speech,
everything one would do

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:29.740
by way of petitioning government
for redress of grievances

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:32.380
is through speech and expression.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.990
And so, the larger protection
of speech and expression

00:13:34.990 --> 00:13:36.530
has meant that the court
hasn't needed to focus

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.700
so much on this particular right.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:39.820
- [Kim] Is there anything that you feel

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:43.420
people commonly misunderstand
about the First Amendment,

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:45.943
what it encompasses and what it does not?

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:49.460
- [Erwin] One of the most
important misunderstandings

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:52.430
about the First Amendment
is people fail to realize

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:55.130
that it, like all rights
in the Constitution,

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:56.860
apply only to the government.

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:59.720
Before I took my current
job, I was a professor

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.070
at Duke University in
Durham, North Carolina.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:03.970
Duke is a private university.

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:07.180
If while I was there, I had
criticized the president

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:09.550
of the university and he
would have ordered me fired,

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:12.350
I could not have sued
him or Duke University

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:14.030
for violating my free speech rights.

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.100
The First Amendment doesn't apply

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:17.240
if it's a private university.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:19.200
But now I'm with the
University of California,

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:20.360
a state university.

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.240
If I were to give a speech criticizing

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:23.550
the president of the university

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.040
or the chancellor of my campus

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:27.680
and I was to be fired for
doing that, I could sue.

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:29.040
I would sue.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:30.730
Since this is a public university,

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.180
the First Amendment applies.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:34.370
- [Michael] So I think
the really dangerous thing

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:36.637
in our times is that

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:42.770
many people believe that they
have some kind of a right

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:47.510
not to hear opinions
that they find offensive.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:50.540
Certainly college campuses are filled

00:14:50.540 --> 00:14:52.750
with controversies of this sort.

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:57.060
This is something that our Constitution

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:58.500
was designed to prevent.

00:14:58.500 --> 00:15:01.900
Free speech can inflict offense.

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:05.140
Sometimes it can be hurtful and insulting.

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:09.280
But we as a nation have decided

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:11.710
that it is better to put up with that

00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:14.420
so that we can all be
free to express ourselves,

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:16.660
to criticize the government,

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:19.640
to urge the religious and scientific

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.663
and artistic ideas that we have.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:25.610
And that it's more important for all of us

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:28.040
to be able to do that than it is

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:31.946
to be able to retreat to safe spaces,

00:15:31.946 --> 00:15:35.690
and require other people to shut up.

00:15:35.690 --> 00:15:37.680
- [Kim] So we've learned
that the rights protected

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:41.120
in the First Amendment derive
from the historical context

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:44.860
of restricted speech, press,
and religion in Europe

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:47.610
that the framers wished to
avoid in the United States.

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:50.100
Freedom of religion
includes both the freedom

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:53.500
not to participate in religion
and the freedom to practice

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.360
whichever religion you choose.

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:58.160
Freedom of speech extends to all forms

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:01.030
of freedom of expression, not just words.

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:03.840
But there are limits to
what counts as free speech.

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:05.760
To learn more about the First Amendment,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.770
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.810
Interactive Constitution
and Khan Academy's resources

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.823
on U.S. government and politics.

